# DEBUG_PAGEALLOC — the instrument that faults #72's WRITER, on by default until
# #72 is attributed. Same lifecycle as gemini-memdebug.config's PAGE_POISONING.
#
# WHY IT IS NEEDED
#
# #72 is a wild pointer whose writer has never been identified. All eighteen
# captures are of the next READER tripping over damage done minutes or hours
# earlier, so every one of them yields a bare fault address and no culprit.
# DEBUG_PAGEALLOC unmaps freed pages, so the write faults at the instant it
# happens, with a call trace naming the code that did it.
#
# The standing objection was that a fault it caught would still have to be read
# somewhere. That is gone twice over: pstore was never broken (2026-08-27), and
# the printing path is measured good — the identical fault taken deliberately
# from process, workqueue and softirq context prints its report in full
# (2026-08-28, tools/oopsgen/).
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, MEASURED RATHER THAN FEARED
#
# I nearly did not turn this on, reasoning that unmapping every freed page would
# make somebody's desktop several times slower. That was wrong, and the numbers
# are the reason it is on. #103 against the #100 baseline, same board, same
# governor (04-docs/bench/):
#
#   single-thread compute  cpu8 1126 vs 1128 Mops/s      -0.2%
#                          cpu4  689 vs  689             0
#   memcpy                 cpu8 3247 vs 3457 MB/s        -6%
#   process spawn          2793 vs 2592 us               +8%
#   process spawn, cpu8    1956 vs 1641 us               +19%
#   4-way parallel wall    1247 vs 1241 ms               ~0
#
# Compute is untouched; the cost lands on page-table work, which is what spawn
# and memcpy measure. Against a bug that takes the machine down for 5-20 minutes
# at a time, that is cheap.
#
# HOW TO DRIVE IT
#
# Pair with gemini-72-soak.service. The detector for the corruption is a page
# cache walk, but what plants it needs TIME — sixty walks in four minutes on a
# fresh boot found nothing, and neither did thirteen after a real CONSYS
# func-off. One walk every three minutes, for hours, is the shape that matches
# how the bug actually shows up.
#
# REMOVE THIS FILE once #72 is attributed.
CONFIG_DEBUG_PAGEALLOC=y
CONFIG_DEBUG_PAGEALLOC_ENABLE_DEFAULT=y
